          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Who does guidance describe as a priority group for influenza vaccination?",
          "answer": "The retrieved guidance does not give a list of priority groups for influenza vaccination, so I can't name them from these sources.\n\nThe main source, the national immunization and infectious diseases guidance, says United States guidance recommends **routine annual vaccination for everyone aged 6 months and older who has no contraindication**. It does not restrict vaccination to high-risk groups. Vaccination before the end of October is the usual aim in the northern hemisphere, and later vaccination is still worthwhile while the virus circulates. (Source: *Influenza: annual vaccination and the timing of antiviral treatment*.)\n\nTwo other documents mention vaccination for people who already have a condition:\n- Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) guidance from a global respiratory strategy report lists influenza vaccination among measures that reduce flare-ups.\n- Coronary artery disease guidance from a major cardiology consensus lists annual influenza vaccination as a secondary prevention measure.\n\nA general clinical reference on influenza says complications can worsen asthma, COPD, heart failure or diabetes. It does not say these groups are priority groups for vaccination.\n\nThe disagreement in the retrieved material is about antiviral treatment in low-risk outpatients, not about who should be vaccinated. I found no retrieved passage where authorities disagree on vaccine priority groups. The retrieved text also does not say how strong the evidence is for universal vaccination.\n\nIf you want to know which groups a particular health system prioritises, ask a clinician or check your national or local immunization schedule.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
